  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure tracking just north of the forecast area and a ridge \nbuilding across the region will briefly provide for a pleasant day \ntoday. Partly to mostly sunny skies with highs in the mid 70s to low \n80s expected today. Winds will also be light with the sfc high just \nto the north. \n\nThe weather will become unsettled starting Sunday morning/afternoon \nas a sfc low tracks over the area. The sfc low will track east \nwithin a shortwave trough extending south out of Canada where \nanother sfc low will be found near James Bay. Out ahead of the \napproaching sfc low, moisture will surge north, causing PWat values \nto increase to around or above 2.00\" by Sunday afternoon/evening. \nWith the forcing form the passing sfc low and trailing cold front, \nshowers will become better organized over the area, with heavy \nrainfall possible at times. This will especially be the case for any \nthunderstorms that may develop. Thunderstorm potential as of right \nnow looks minimal, but if shower development is delay to later in \nthe afternoon and there is a bit more clearing, then some better \ninstability may develop and increase the potential for some \nthunderstorms. Timing among guidance is still in disagreement \nbetween the morning and afternoon for onset time of the \nshowers/storms. More persistent showers will continue through the \nfirst half of the overnight hours on Sunday night before shifting \noff to the east. Scattered to isolated showers and a possible \nthunderstorm will linger through the day on Monday. Rainfall amounts \nare split among the guidance with a stripe of heavier rainfall more \nconsistently in the Pennsylvania portion of the forecast area. This \nis the area where better forcing is expected with the moisture in \nplace. Rainfall of up to around an inch is possible for the northern \nhalf of the forecast area, and closer to an inch and a half for the \nsouthern half is possible. But again, rainfall amounts from guidance \nare trending lower.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure will track across the Ohio Valley and to the Eat Coast \nfrom Tuesday through Wednesday. This will allow for quiet weather \nfor the majority of both of these days. Afternoon highs in the mid \n70s to low 80s can also be expected. \n\nAs of the latest guidance, a cold front will extend south from a \npotent sfc low up near Hudson Bay. This front will track east across \nthe area late Wednesday through Thursday, bringing another round of \nshowers. Some guidance brings the front through quick, while other \nguidance is a bit slower with the passage as a sfc low develops and \nmoves along the boundary. This far out, guidance is sure to flip \nback and forth in time.",
